BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:SFCS at 10: Moonlight (2016)
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261209T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261209T211500
DTSTAMP:20261210T030000Z
UID:sfcs-10-moonlight-2026-12-09-19:00:00-08:00
DESCRIPTION:Special introduction by Timothy Hall of The People’s Critic
 \n\nA timeless story of human connection and self-discovery\, Moonlight ch
 ronicles the life of a young black man from childhood to adulthood as he s
 truggles to find his place in the world while growing up in a rough neighb
 orhood of Miami. At once a vital portrait of contemporary African-American
  life and an intensely personal and poetic meditation on identity\, family
 \, friendship\, and love\, Moonlight is a groundbreaking piece of cinema t
 hat reverberates with deep compassion and universal truths. Anchored by ex
 traordinary performances from a tremendous ensemble cast\, Barry Jenkins
 ’s staggering\, singular vision is profoundly moving in its portrayal of
  the moments\, people\, and unknowable forces that shape our lives and mak
 e us who we are.\n\nBarry Jenkins\, 2016\, United States\, 111 min\, in En
 glish\n\nhttps://nwfilmforum.org/films/sfcs-10-moonlight/\n\nEstimated sta
 rt: 19:20
LOCATION:Northwest Film Forum\, 1515 12th Ave\, Seattle\, WA 98122
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Seattle LGBTQ+ Center Presents: We Were Here (2011)
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261202T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261202T205000
DTSTAMP:20261203T030000Z
UID:seattle-lgbtq-center-presents-we-were-here-2026-12-02-19:00:00-08:00
DESCRIPTION:We Were Here is the first documentary to take a deep and refle
 ctive look back at the arrival and impact of AIDS in San Francisco. It exp
 lores how the City’s inhabitants were affected by\, and how they respond
 ed to\, that calamitous epidemic.\n\nThough a San Francisco-based story\, 
 We Were Here extends beyond San Francisco and beyond AIDS itself. It speak
 s to our capacity as individuals to rise to the occasion\, and to the incr
 edible power of a community coming together with love\, compassion\, and d
 etermination.\n\nFilmmakers David Weissman and Bill Weber co-directed the 
 2001 documentary\, The Cockettes\, chronicling San Francisco’s legendary
  theater troupe of hippies and drag queens\, 1969 – 1972. We Were Here r
 evisits San Francisco a decade later\, as its flourishing gay community is
  hit with an unimaginable disaster.\n\nDavid Weissman\, United States\, 20
 11\, 90 min\, in English\n\nhttps://nwfilmforum.org/films/seattle-lgbtq-ce
 nter-presents-we-were-here/\n\nEstimated start: 19:20
LOCATION:Northwest Film Forum\, 1515 12th Ave\, Seattle\, WA 98122
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Singles (1992)
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20270126T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20270126T210000
DTSTAMP:20270127T030000Z
UID:singles-2027-01-26-19:00:00-08:00
DESCRIPTION:Set amidst the burgeoning Seattle alternative music scene of t
 he early Nineties\, Singles follows a group of twentysomethings as they tr
 y to find love and try to come to terms with their passage into adulthood.
  Arranged as an episodic comedy\, the film follows a group of friends who 
 live in the same apartment building and hang out at the same coffee shop.
 \n\nThe central couple is Steve Dunne (Campbell Scott) and Linda Powell (K
 yra Sedgwick)\, a pair who meet at an Alice In Chains concert and eventual
 ly fall in love. Singles follows the tumultuous relationship between Steve
  and Linda and their friendship with Janet Livermore (Bridget Fonda)\, who
  is trying to win the affection of grunge-rocker Cliff Poncier (Matt Dillo
 n).\n\nThe film also has a number of cameos\, including actors Eric Stoltz
 \, Tom Skerritt\, Peter Horton\, director Tim Burton and the film’s auth
 or/director\, Cameron Crowe. From the musical side of the fence\, Singles 
 features appearances by Sub Pop executive Bruce Pavitt\, musicians Chris C
 ornell (Soundgarden)\, Pat DiNizio (Smithereens)\, Tad (Tad)\, and Pearl J
 am’s Eddie Vedder\, Jeff Ament\, and Stone Gossard\, who play Dillon’s
  backing band\, Citizen Dick.\n\nCameron Crowe\, United States\, 1992\, 99
  min\, in English\n\nhttps://nwfilmforum.org/films/singles/\n\nEstimated s
 tart: 19:20
LOCATION:Northwest Film Forum\, 1515 12th Ave\, Seattle\, WA 98122
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Apple (1980)
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261207T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261207T210000
DTSTAMP:20261208T030000Z
UID:the-apple-2026-12-07-19:00:00-08:00
DESCRIPTION:Have you always wished that Adam and Eve had their chance to s
 hine on America’s Got Talent? The Apple is just the movie for you! Follo
 wing the trials and tribulations of siblings Bibi and Alphie as they navig
 ate the deeply corrupt music industry in the distant future of 1994\, The 
 Apple is a fable that warns us all of the dangers that await when we unque
 stionably put stickers on our heads. Featuring sets\, costumes\, and produ
 ctions that far surpass any other aspect of this film\, The Apple is a mus
 ical that proves anyone can make a movie\, especially if they can blow eno
 ugh money on everything to make it pretty.\n\nMenahem Golan\, United State
 s & Germany\, 1980\, 97 min\, in English\n\nhttps://nwfilmforum.org/films/
 the-apple/\n\nEstimated start: 19:20
LOCATION:Northwest Film Forum\, 1515 12th Ave\, Seattle\, WA 98122
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
